/-!
# Algebraic sets, radicals and coordinate groups over a group

Let `H` be a group receiving the coefficients `G` through `ι : G →* H`. A system of equations
over `G` in the variables `X` is a subset `S ⊆ G[X]`, and its solutions in `H` form the
*algebraic set* `V_H(S) ⊆ H^X`. Conversely the equations vanishing on a set of points
`Y ⊆ H^X` form a normal subgroup `I_H(Y)` of `G[X]`, the *ideal* of `Y`. These two operations are
the upper and lower polars of the relation `eval ι v s = 1`, so they form an antitone Galois
connection. The *radical* `Rad_H(S) = I_H(V_H(S))` of `S` is the largest
system with the same solutions as `S`; it contains the normal closure of `S`, generally strictly.

A `G`-homomorphism from a quotient `G[X] ⧸ N` to `H` is the same as a point of `H^X` at which
every equation of `N` vanishes. Quotienting `G[X]` by the radical gives the *coordinate group*
`G_{R(S)}` of `S`, while quotienting by the normal closure gives the group `G_S` presented by the
system itself; there is a surjection `G_S →* G_{R(S)}` compatible with the coefficients. When `ι`
is injective and `S` has a solution in `H`, both coefficient maps are injective, so `G_{R(S)}` is a
`G`-group; conversely `G_{R(S)}` is a `G`-group only in this case. Evaluation at a solution `v`
maps `G_{R(S)}` onto the subgroup of `H` generated by the coefficients and `v`, isomorphically when
`v` is the only solution.

The radical genuinely depends on the group `H` in which the solutions are taken, and differs from
the normal closure: over a torsion-free `H` with injective coefficients, the radical of the
one-variable system `x ^ n = 1` (`n ≠ 0`) is the normal closure of `x`, which does not lie in the
normal closure of `x ^ n` when `n ≠ 1`. At the other extreme, over `F(a, b)` the one-variable
system `{a, b, x}` has no solutions; its radical and its normal closure are both all of
`F(a, b)[x]`, so its group is trivial.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V ι S`: the algebraic set `V_H(S)` of a system `S ⊆ G[X]`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.I ι Y`: the ideal `I_H(Y)`, a normal subgroup of `G[X]`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad ι S`: the radical `Rad_H(S) = I_H(V_H(S))`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup ι S`: the coordinate group `G_{R(S)} = G[X] ⧸ Rad_H(S)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.genericPoint ι S`: the generic point of `S`, the tuple of the
  images of the variables in `G_{R(S)}`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup G S`: the group `G_S = G[X] ⧸ ncl(S)` of the system `S`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup.toCoordGroup ι S`: the surjection `G_S →* G_{R(S)}`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.gHomEquivCentralizer`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.gHomEquivZPowers`: `G`-homomorphisms to `H` from the
  coordinate group of `[x, g] = 1` correspond to the centralizer of `ι g`; over a free group `F`
  with a free generator `a`, those from the coordinate group of `[x, a] = 1` to `F` correspond to
  the powers of `a`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.homOfVSubset`: the surjection `G_{R(T)} →* G_{R(S)}` induced by
  the identity on the variables when `V_H(S) ⊆ V_H(T)`, an isomorphism when the solution sets are
  equal.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.galoisConnection_V_I`: `V` and `I` form an antitone Galois connection.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_normalClosure`, `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.normalClosure_le_Rad`: a system
  and its normal closure have the same solutions, so `ncl(S) ≤ Rad_H(S)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_I_V`: `V(I(V(S))) = V(S)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.preimage_wordMap_V`: inverse images of algebraic sets under word maps
  are algebraic.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.exists_finite_V_eq_of_finite`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.exists_subsingleton_V_eq`: with finitely many points, every system has a
  finite subsystem with the same solutions; with at most one point, for instance with no
  variables, a single equation suffices.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.exists_empty_eq_V_iff`: the empty set is algebraic exactly when some
  coefficient remains nontrivial in `H`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.mul_inv_mul_mem_V`, `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.not_exists_pair_eq_V`: in a
  commutative group an algebraic set is closed under `(u, v, w) ↦ u * v⁻¹ * w`, so two tuples with
  different squares do not form an algebraic set.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.I_singleton_comp`: the ideal of a point with coordinates `ι (g x)` is the
  normal closure of the equations `x (g x)⁻¹`, for injective `ι`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup.coeff_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl_bijective`: the system
  `x (g x)⁻¹`, `x ∈ X`, is radical for injective `ι`, and its group is `G` itself.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_eq_empty_of_inl_mem`, `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad_eq_top_of_V_eq_empty`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup.isEmpty_gHom_iff`: a system containing a constant that is
  nontrivial in `H` has no solutions; the radical of such a system is everything and its group has
  no `G`-homomorphism to `H`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_empty`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.normalClosure_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_top`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup.isEmpty_gHom_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of`: over `F(a, b)` the system
  `{a, b, x}` has no solutions and its group is trivial, with no coefficient-preserving
  homomorphism to `F(a, b)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.GX.quotientHomEquiv`: `G`-homomorphisms from `G[X] ⧸ N` to `H`
  correspond to the points of `V_H(N)`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.GX.exists_gHom_apply_eq_eval`: for `K` presented over `G` by
  `π : G[X] →* K`, a point of `H^X` at which every relation of `K` vanishes defines a
  `G`-homomorphism `K → H` through which evaluation at the point factors.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.homEquiv`, `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.SystemGroup.homEquiv`:
  `G`-homomorphisms from `G_{R(S)}` or from `G_S` to `H` correspond to the solutions of `S` in `H`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.coeff_injective`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.coeff_injective_iff`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.not_injective_coeff_of_V_eq_empty`: `G_{R(S)}` is a `G`-group
  when `S` has a solution in a `G`-group, and in general exactly when `ι` is injective and `S` has
  a solution in `H`; the coordinate group of a system without solutions is not a `G`-group.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.range_evalGHom`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.evalGHom_injective_iff`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.evalGHom_injective`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.eq_evalGHom`: evaluation at a solution `v` maps `G_{R(S)}` onto
  the subgroup generated by the coefficients and `v`, injectively exactly at a generic point; so
  the coordinate group of a single point `v` is that subgroup, and its only `G`-homomorphism to
  `H` is evaluation at `v`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.subsingleton_iff`: `G_{R(S)}` is trivial exactly when
  evaluation at every solution of `S` in `H` is trivial, for instance when `S` has no solution in
  `H` (`TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.subsingleton_of_V_eq_empty`) or `H` is trivial.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.genericPoint_eq_one_iff`: a coordinate of the generic point
  of `S` is trivial exactly when it is trivial at every solution of `S` in `H`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.homOfMapsToWordMap_id`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.CoordGroup.homOfMapsToWordMap_comp`: a tuple of words mapping one
  algebraic set into another induces a coefficient-preserving homomorphism of coordinate groups
  in the opposite direction, contravariantly functorially.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad_pow_eq_normalClosure`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.normalClosure_pow_ne_normalClosure`: over a torsion-free `H` with
  injective coefficients, the radical of `x ^ n` (`n ≠ 0`) is the normal closure of `x`, which
  strictly contains that of `x ^ n` when `n ≠ 1`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad_pow_mul_inl_pow_eq_normalClosure`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.inr_of_mul_inl_notMem_normalClosure_pow_mul_inl`: over a group `H` with
  unique roots and injective coefficients, the radical of the one-variable equation
  `x ^ n * b ^ n` (`n ≠ 0`) is the normal closure of `x * b`. For `n ≠ 1`, the element `x * b`
  does not lie in the normal closure of `x ^ n * d`, whatever the coefficients `b` and `d`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.Rad_pow_eq_I_univ`, `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.inr_of_mem_Rad_pow_iff`: over a
  group `H` all of whose elements have `n`-th power `1`, every point solves `x ^ n = 1` and its
  radical is the ideal of all points; in general `x` lies in the radical of `x ^ n` exactly when
  `H` has no nonidentity element of `n`-th power `1`.
* `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_image_commutatorElement`,
  `TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom.V_singleton_commutatorElement_of`: centralizers of sets of coefficients
  are algebraic, cut out by the commutator equations `[x, m] = 1`; over a free group, the
  solutions of `[x, a] = 1` for a free generator `a` are the powers of `a`.

## References

* G. Baumslag, A. Myasnikov, V. Remeslennikov, *Algebraic geometry over groups I. Algebraic sets
  and ideal theory*, J. Algebra 219 (1999), §1 and §§3.1–3.4
  (Example 4 of §3.2 for algebraic sets in abelian groups).
* O. Kharlampovich, A. Myasnikov, *Implicit function theorem over free groups*, J. Algebra 290
  (2005), §2.4.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom

open Monoid.Coprod Subgroup

variable {G H X : Type*} [Group G] [Group H]

variable (ι : G →* H)

/-! ### Algebraic sets and ideals -/

/-- The algebraic set `V_H(S) ⊆ H^X` of a system of equations `S ⊆ G[X]`: the points `v : X → H`
at which every equation of `S` evaluates to `1`. -/
def V (S : Set (GX G X)) : Set (X → H) :=
  upperPolar (fun s v => eval ι v s = 1) S

/-- The ideal `I_H(Y)` of a set of points `Y ⊆ H^X`: the equations of `G[X]` vanishing at every
point of `Y`. It is a normal subgroup of `G[X]`. -/
def I (Y : Set (X → H)) : Subgroup (GX G X) where
  carrier := lowerPolar (fun s v => eval ι v s = 1) Y
  mul_mem' ha hb v hv := by simp [ha hv, hb hv]
  one_mem' _ _ := map_one _
  inv_mem' ha v hv := by simp [ha hv]

variable {ι} {S T : Set (GX G X)} {Y Z : Set (X → H)} {v : X → H} {w : GX G X}

@[simp]
theorem mem_V : v ∈ V ι S ↔ ∀ s ∈ S, eval ι v s = 1 := Iff.rfl

@[simp]
theorem mem_I : w ∈ I ι Y ↔ ∀ v ∈ Y, eval ι v w = 1 := Iff.rfl

instance (Y : Set (X → H)) : (I ι Y).Normal where
  conj_mem w hw g v hv := by simp [(mem_I.1 hw) v hv]

/-- The ideal of `Y` is the intersection of the kernels of evaluation at the points of `Y`. -/
theorem I_eq_iInf_ker (Y : Set (X → H)) : I ι Y = ⨅ v ∈ Y, (eval ι v).ker := by
  ext w
  simp

/-- A system vanishes on a set of points if and only if the points solve the system. -/
theorem subset_I_iff : S ⊆ I ι Y ↔ Y ⊆ V ι S :=
  subset_upperPolar_iff_subset_lowerPolar.symm

variable (ι) in
/-- Algebraic sets and ideals form an antitone Galois connection between systems of equations in
`G[X]` and sets of points in `H^X`. -/
theorem galoisConnection_V_I :
    GaloisConnection (fun S : Set (GX G X) => OrderDual.toDual (V ι S))
      (fun Y => (I ι (OrderDual.ofDual Y) : Set (GX G X))) :=
  gc_upperPolar_lowerPolar _

variable (ι) in
theorem V_antitone : Antitone (V ι : Set (GX G X) → Set (X → H)) :=
  upperPolar_anti _

variable (ι) in
theorem I_antitone : Antitone (I ι : Set (X → H) → Subgroup (GX G X)) :=
  fun _ _ h => lowerPolar_anti _ h

theorem subset_V_I (Y : Set (X → H)) : Y ⊆ V ι (I ι Y) :=
  subset_upperPolar_lowerPolar _ Y

/-- The algebraic set of the equations vanishing on an algebraic set is that algebraic set. -/
@[simp]
theorem V_I_V (S : Set (GX G X)) : V ι (I ι (V ι S)) = V ι S :=
  upperPolar_lowerPolar_upperPolar _ S

@[simp]
theorem I_V_I (Y : Set (X → H)) : I ι (V ι (I ι Y)) = I ι Y :=
  SetLike.coe_injective (lowerPolar_upperPolar_lowerPolar _ Y)

@[simp]
theorem V_empty : V ι (∅ : Set (GX G X)) = Set.univ :=
  upperPolar_empty _

@[simp]
theorem I_empty : I ι (∅ : Set (X → H)) = ⊤ :=
  SetLike.coe_injective ((lowerPolar_empty _).trans coe_top.symm)

@[simp]
theorem V_union (S T : Set (GX G X)) : V ι (S ∪ T) = V ι S ∩ V ι T :=
  upperPolar_union _ S T

@[simp]
theorem V_iUnion {κ : Sort*} (S : κ → Set (GX G X)) : V ι (⋃ k, S k) = ⋂ k, V ι (S k) :=
  upperPolar_iUnion _ S

/-- Pulling an algebraic set back along a tuple of words substitutes those words into its
equations. -/
theorem preimage_wordMap_V {Y : Type*} (w : Y → GX G X) (S : Set (GX G Y)) :
    wordMap ι w ⁻¹' V ι S =
      V ι ((eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w) '' S) := by
  ext v
  simp only [Set.mem_preimage, mem_V, Set.mem_image]
  constructor
  · intro hv s hs
    obtain ⟨t, ht, rfl⟩ := hs
    rw [← MonoidHom.comp_apply, eval_comp_eval]
    exact hv t ht
  · intro hv t ht
    have := hv _ ⟨t, ht, rfl⟩
    rwa [← MonoidHom.comp_apply, eval_comp_eval] at this

/-- Changing the coefficients of a system along `f : K →* G` and renaming its variables along
`e : Y → X` pulls its algebraic set back along `e`: a point `v` solves the image system through `ι`
exactly when `v ∘ e` solves the system through `ι ∘ f`. -/
@[simp]
theorem V_image_map {K Y : Type*} [Group K] (f : K →* G) (e : Y → X) (S : Set (GX K Y)) :
    V ι (Monoid.Coprod.map f (FreeGroup.map e) '' S) = (· ∘ e) ⁻¹' V (ι.comp f) S := by
  ext v
  simp

/-- Changing the coefficients of an equation along `f : K →* G` and renaming its variables along
`e : Y → X` pulls its algebraic set back along `e`. -/
@[simp]
theorem V_singleton_map {K Y : Type*} [Group K] (f : K →* G) (e : Y → X) (w : GX K Y) :
    V ι {Monoid.Coprod.map f (FreeGroup.map e) w} = (· ∘ e) ⁻¹' V (ι.comp f) {w} := by
  ext v
  simp

/-- The inverse image of a solution set under the tuple word map is the solution set
of the image system. -/
theorem preimage_wordMap_V_eq_V_image {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (T : Set (GX G Y)) :
    wordMap ι (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) ⁻¹' V ι T = V ι (σ '' T) := by
  calc
    wordMap ι (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) ⁻¹' V ι T =
        V ι ((eval (inl : G →* GX G X)
          (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y)))) '' T) := preimage_wordMap_V _ _
    _ = V ι (σ '' T) := congrArg (fun f : GX G Y →* GX G X => V ι (f '' T))
      (eval_eq_of_comp_inl σ.comp_coeff)

/-- A word map sends solutions to solutions of the inverse-image system. -/
theorem wordMap_mem_V {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (S : Set (GX G X)) (v : X → H) (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    wordMap ι (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) v ∈ V ι (σ ⁻¹' S) := by
  have h := (Set.ext_iff.mp (preimage_wordMap_V_eq_V_image ι σ (σ ⁻¹' S))) v
  exact h.mpr (V_antitone ι (Set.image_preimage_subset σ S) hv)

/-- A tuple solves `S` exactly when its word map solves the inverse-image system. -/
theorem wordMap_mem_V_iff {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (hσ : Function.Surjective σ) (S : Set (GX G X)) (v : X → H) :
    wordMap ι (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) v ∈ V ι (σ ⁻¹' S) ↔ v ∈ V ι S := by
  simpa only [Set.mem_preimage, Set.image_preimage_eq S hσ] using
    (Set.ext_iff.mp (preimage_wordMap_V_eq_V_image ι σ (σ ⁻¹' S))) v

/-- The singleton of a tuple of coefficients is the solution set of the equations specifying
all its coordinates. -/
theorem singleton_comp_eq_V (g : X → G) :
    ({(ι ∘ g : X → H)} : Set (X → H)) =
      V ι (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹) := by
  ext v
  simp only [Set.mem_singleton_iff, mem_V, Set.mem_range]
  constructor
  · rintro rfl s ⟨x, rfl⟩
    simp
  · intro hv
    funext x
    have hx := hv _ ⟨x, rfl⟩
    simpa only [Function.comp_apply, map_mul, map_inv, eval_inl, eval_inr_of,
      mul_inv_eq_one] using hx

/-- If a coefficient becomes trivial in `H`, every point is a solution of the equation with that
constant. -/
theorem V_singleton_inl_eq_univ {g : G} (hg : ι g = 1) :
    V ι ({inl g} : Set (GX G X)) = Set.univ := by
  ext v
  simp [hg]

/-- If some coefficient remains nontrivial in `H`, an equation with that constant has no
solutions. -/
theorem V_singleton_inl_eq_empty {g : G} (hg : ι g ≠ 1) :
    V ι ({inl g} : Set (GX G X)) = ∅ := by
  ext v
  simp [hg]

/-- A system containing a constant that remains nontrivial in `H` has no solutions. -/
theorem V_eq_empty_of_inl_mem {g : G} (hg : ι g ≠ 1) (hS : inl g ∈ S) : V ι S = ∅ :=
  Set.subset_empty_iff.1 <|
    (V_antitone ι (Set.singleton_subset_iff.2 hS)).trans (V_singleton_inl_eq_empty hg).subset

open scoped commutatorElement in
/-- A point solves the equation `[x, y] = 1` if and only if its coordinates at `x` and `y`
commute. -/
theorem mem_V_commutator_iff {x y : X} :
    v ∈ V ι {⁅(inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X), inr (FreeGroup.of y)⁆} ↔ Commute (v x) (v y) := by
  simp [commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute]

/-- If a coefficient `c` remains nontrivial in `H`, an equation `u⁻¹ c u = 1` saying that a
conjugate of `c` is trivial has no solutions; `V_singleton_inl_eq_empty` is the case `u = 1`. In
particular, for a variable `z`, the equation `z⁻¹ c z = 1` has no solutions. -/
theorem V_singleton_inv_mul_inl_mul_eq_empty (u : GX G X) {c : G} (hc : ι c ≠ 1) :
    V ι {u⁻¹ * inl c * u} = ∅ := by
  ext v
  simp [mul_eq_one_iff_eq_inv, hc]

/-- If no coefficient survives in `H`, the trivial tuple solves every system. -/
theorem one_mem_V (hι : ∀ g, ι g = 1) (S : Set (GX G X)) : (1 : X → H) ∈ V ι S := by
  obtain rfl : ι = 1 := MonoidHom.ext hι
  simp

/-- The empty set is algebraic exactly when some coefficient remains nontrivial in `H`. -/
theorem exists_empty_eq_V_iff :
    (∃ S : Set (GX G X), (∅ : Set (X → H)) = V ι S) ↔ ∃ g, ι g ≠ 1 := by
  refine ⟨?_, fun ⟨g, hg⟩ => ⟨{inl g}, (V_singleton_inl_eq_empty hg).symm⟩⟩
  rintro ⟨S, hS⟩
  by_contra! h
  exact Set.notMem_empty _ (hS ▸ one_mem_V h S)

/-- Over a trivial group every tuple solves every system. -/
theorem V_eq_univ_of_subsingleton [Subsingleton H] (S : Set (GX G X)) : V ι S = Set.univ :=
  Set.eq_univ_of_forall fun _ => mem_V.2 fun _ _ => Subsingleton.elim _ _

/-- Over a torsion-free group in which no coefficient survives, a system in at most one variable
with a nontrivial solution is solved by every tuple: each equation is then a power of the
variable. -/
theorem V_eq_univ_of_mem_of_ne_one [IsMulTorsionFree H] [Subsingleton X] (hι : ∀ g, ι g = 1)
    (hvS : v ∈ V ι S) (hv : v ≠ 1) : V ι S = Set.univ := by
  obtain rfl : ι = 1 := MonoidHom.ext hι
  obtain ⟨x₀, hx₀⟩ := Function.ne_iff.1 hv
  -- Every word evaluates to a fixed power of the value of the variable.
  have key (u : X → H) : eval (1 : G →* H) u =
      (zpowersHom H (u x₀)).comp (eval 1 fun _ => Multiplicative.ofAdd 1) :=
    hom_ext (MonoidHom.ext fun g => by simp)
      (FreeGroup.ext_hom _ _ fun x => by simp [Subsingleton.elim x x₀])
  refine Set.eq_univ_of_forall fun u => mem_V.2 fun s hs => ?_
  have h := mem_V.1 hvS s hs
  rw [key, MonoidHom.comp_apply, zpowersHom_apply,
    IsMulTorsionFree.zpow_eq_one_iff_right hx₀] at h
  rw [key, MonoidHom.comp_apply, zpowersHom_apply, h, zpow_zero]

/-- In a commutative group an algebraic set is closed under `(u, v, w) ↦ u * v⁻¹ * w`, which
is the sense in which a nonempty algebraic set is a coset of a subgroup of tuples. -/
theorem mul_inv_mul_mem_V {A : Type*} [CommGroup A] {ι : G →* A} {u v w : X → A} (hu : u ∈ V ι S)
    (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (hw : w ∈ V ι S) : u * v⁻¹ * w ∈ V ι S :=
  mem_V.2 fun s hs => by
    simp [eval_mul_inv_mul, mem_V.1 hu s hs, mem_V.1 hv s hs, mem_V.1 hw s hs]

/-- In a commutative group a set of two tuples with different squares is not algebraic: an
algebraic set containing `a` and `b` also contains `a * b⁻¹ * a`. -/
theorem not_exists_pair_eq_V {A : Type*} [CommGroup A] (ι : G →* A) {a b : X → A}
    (hab : a ^ 2 ≠ b ^ 2) : ¬∃ S : Set (GX G X), ({a, b} : Set (X → A)) = V ι S := by
  rintro ⟨S, hS⟩
  have ha : a ∈ V ι S := hS ▸ Set.mem_insert a {b}
  have hb : b ∈ V ι S := hS ▸ Set.mem_insert_of_mem a rfl
  have h : a * b⁻¹ * a ∈ ({a, b} : Set (X → A)) := hS ▸ mul_inv_mul_mem_V ha hb ha
  rcases h with h | h
  · exact hab (by rw [mul_inv_eq_one.1 (mul_eq_right.1 h)])
  · have h' : a * a = b * b := by
      rw [← mul_inv_eq_iff_eq_mul, mul_right_comm]
      exact h
    exact hab (by rw [pow_two, pow_two, h'])

@[simp]
theorem I_union (Y Z : Set (X → H)) : I ι (Y ∪ Z) = I ι Y ⊓ I ι Z :=
  SetLike.coe_injective ((lowerPolar_union _ Y Z).trans (coe_inf (I ι Y) (I ι Z)).symm)

@[simp]
theorem I_iUnion {κ : Sort*} (Y : κ → Set (X → H)) : I ι (⋃ k, Y k) = ⨅ k, I ι (Y k) :=
  SetLike.coe_injective ((lowerPolar_iUnion _ Y).trans (coe_iInf (S := fun k => I ι (Y k))).symm)

/-- Conjugating an equation does not change its solutions. -/
@[simp]
theorem V_singleton_conj (u w : GX G X) : V ι {u * w * u⁻¹} = V ι {w} := by
  ext v
  simp

/-- A system and its normal closure have the same solutions. -/
@[simp]
theorem V_normalClosure (S : Set (GX G X)) : V ι (normalClosure S : Set (GX G X)) = V ι S := by
  refine (V_antitone ι subset_normalClosure).antisymm fun v hv => ?_
  have : normalClosure S ≤ (eval ι v).ker := normalClosure_le_normal fun s hs => mem_V.1 hv s hs
  exact mem_V.2 fun s hs => this hs

/-- A `G`-homomorphism maps solutions of a system to solutions of the same system. -/
theorem GHom.comp_mem_V {K : Type*} [Group K] {κ : G →* K} (φ : GHom ι κ) (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    φ ∘ v ∈ V κ S :=
  mem_V.2 fun s hs => by rw [← map_eval, mem_V.1 hv s hs, map_one]

/-! ### Finite subsystems -/

/-- A homomorphism `m : G[X] →* G'[Y]` together with a map of points `θ` compatible with
evaluation carries finite subsystems of `m(S)` with the same solutions back to finite subsystems
of `S` with the same solutions. -/
theorem exists_finite_V_eq_of_map {G' Y : Type*} [Group G'] {ι : G →* H} {ι' : G' →* H}
    (m : GX G X →* GX G' Y) (θ : (X → H) → (Y → H))
    (hθ : ∀ v s, eval ι' (θ v) (m s) = eval ι v s) {S : Set (GX G X)}
    (h : ∃ T ⊆ m '' S, T.Finite ∧ V ι' T = V ι' (m '' S)) :
    ∃ S₀ ⊆ S, S₀.Finite ∧ V ι S₀ = V ι S := by
  obtain ⟨S₀, hS₀, hfin, hV⟩ := Set.exists_subset_image_finite_and.1 h
  have key (T : Set (GX G X)) : V ι T = θ ⁻¹' V ι' (m '' T) := by
    ext v
    simp [hθ]
  exact ⟨S₀, hS₀, hfin, by rw [key, key, hV]⟩

/-- If there are only finitely many points `X → H`, every system is equivalent to a finite
subsystem: one equation failing at each non-solution suffices. -/
theorem exists_finite_V_eq_of_finite {ι : G →* H} [Finite (X → H)] (S : Set (GX G X)) :
    ∃ S₀ ⊆ S, S₀.Finite ∧ V ι S₀ = V ι S := by
  have hw (v : ↥(V ι S)ᶜ) : ∃ s ∈ S, eval ι v s ≠ 1 := by
    have hv := v.2
    simpa only [Set.mem_compl_iff, mem_V, not_forall, exists_prop] using hv
  choose w hwS hw using hw
  have hsub : Set.range w ⊆ S := Set.range_subset_iff.2 hwS
  refine ⟨Set.range w, hsub, Set.finite_range w, (V_antitone ι hsub).antisymm' fun v hv => ?_⟩
  by_contra hvS
  exact hw ⟨v, hvS⟩ (mem_V.1 hv _ ⟨⟨v, hvS⟩, rfl⟩)

/-- If there is at most one point `X → H`, for instance when there are no variables, a single
equation suffices: a system with a solution is equivalent to the empty subsystem, and a system
without one is equivalent to any of its equations that fails at the point. -/
theorem exists_subsingleton_V_eq {ι : G →* H} [Subsingleton (X → H)] (S : Set (GX G X)) :
    ∃ S₀ ⊆ S, S₀.Subsingleton ∧ V ι S₀ = V ι S := by
  by_cases hS : ∃ s ∈ S, ∃ v, eval ι v s ≠ 1
  · obtain ⟨s, hs, v, hv⟩ := hS
    have hs₀ : V ι {s} = ∅ :=
      Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem.2 fun u hu => hv (Subsingleton.elim v u ▸ mem_V.1 hu s rfl)
    refine ⟨{s}, Set.singleton_subset_iff.2 hs, Set.subsingleton_singleton, ?_⟩
    rw [hs₀, eq_comm, ← Set.subset_empty_iff, ← hs₀]
    exact V_antitone ι (Set.singleton_subset_iff.2 hs)
  · refine ⟨∅, Set.empty_subset S, Set.subsingleton_empty, ?_⟩
    simp only [not_exists, not_and, not_not] at hS
    rw [V_empty]
    exact (Set.eq_univ_of_forall fun v => mem_V.2 fun s hs => hS s hs v).symm

/-! ### Radicals -/

variable (ι) in
/-- The radical `Rad_H(S) = I_H(V_H(S))` of a system `S ⊆ G[X]` over `H`: the equations satisfied
by every solution of `S` in `H`. -/
def Rad (S : Set (GX G X)) : Subgroup (GX G X) :=
  I ι (V ι S)

theorem Rad_def (S : Set (GX G X)) : Rad ι S = I ι (V ι S) := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem mem_Rad : w ∈ Rad ι S ↔ ∀ v ∈ V ι S, eval ι v w = 1 := Iff.rfl

instance (S : Set (GX G X)) : (Rad ι S).Normal :=
  inferInstanceAs (I ι (V ι S)).Normal

theorem subset_Rad (S : Set (GX G X)) : S ⊆ Rad ι S :=
  subset_I_iff.2 subset_rfl

/-- The radical of `S` contains the normal closure of `S`. -/
theorem normalClosure_le_Rad (S : Set (GX G X)) : normalClosure S ≤ Rad ι S :=
  normalClosure_le_normal (subset_Rad S)

variable (ι) in
theorem Rad_monotone : Monotone (Rad ι : Set (GX G X) → Subgroup (GX G X)) :=
  fun _ _ h => I_antitone ι (V_antitone ι h)

@[simp]
theorem V_Rad (S : Set (GX G X)) : V ι (Rad ι S : Set (GX G X)) = V ι S :=
  V_I_V S

/-- Taking radicals is idempotent. -/
@[simp]
theorem Rad_Rad (S : Set (GX G X)) : Rad ι (Rad ι S : Set (GX G X)) = Rad ι S := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_Rad, Rad_def]

/-- Conjugating an equation does not change its radical. -/
@[simp]
theorem Rad_singleton_conj (u w : GX G X) : Rad ι {u * w * u⁻¹} = Rad ι {w} := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_singleton_conj, Rad_def]

@[simp]
theorem Rad_normalClosure (S : Set (GX G X)) :
    Rad ι (normalClosure S : Set (GX G X)) = Rad ι S := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_normalClosure, Rad_def]

@[simp]
theorem Rad_empty : Rad ι (∅ : Set (GX G X)) = I ι Set.univ := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_empty]

/-- The radical of `S` is the whole of `G[X]` if and only if evaluation at every solution of `S` in
`H` is trivial. -/
theorem Rad_eq_top_iff : Rad ι S = ⊤ ↔ ∀ v ∈ V ι S, eval ι v = 1 := by
  simp only [eq_top_iff', mem_Rad, MonoidHom.ext_iff, MonoidHom.one_apply]
  exact ⟨fun h v hv w => h w v hv, fun h w v hv => h v hv w⟩

/-- The radical of a system without solutions in `H` is the whole of `G[X]`. -/
theorem Rad_eq_top_of_V_eq_empty (hS : V ι S = ∅) : Rad ι S = ⊤ := by
  rw [Rad_def, hS, I_empty]

/-- For injective coefficients, the ideal of a point `ι ∘ g` with coordinates in the image of `G`
is the normal closure of the equations `x (g x)⁻¹`, `x ∈ X`. -/
theorem I_singleton_comp (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : X → G) :
    I ι {ι ∘ g} = normalClosure (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹) := by
  refine le_antisymm (fun w hw => ?_) (normalClosure_le_normal ?_)
  · -- Modulo the normal closure `N` of the equations `x (g x)⁻¹`, every element of `G[X]` is
    -- congruent to the coefficient given by evaluation at `g`.
    set N := normalClosure (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹)
    have hπ : QuotientGroup.mk' N =
        ((QuotientGroup.mk' N).comp inl).comp (eval (.id G) g) := by
      refine hom_ext (MonoidHom.ext fun _ => by simp) (FreeGroup.ext_hom _ _ fun x => ?_)
      have : inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹ ∈ N := subset_normalClosure ⟨x, rfl⟩
      simpa [QuotientGroup.eq_iff_div_mem, div_eq_mul_inv] using this
    have hw : eval (.id G) g w = 1 := by
      apply hι
      rw [map_one, ← MonoidHom.comp_apply, MonoidHom.comp_eval, MonoidHom.comp_id]
      exact mem_I.1 hw _ rfl
    rw [← QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff (N := N), ← QuotientGroup.mk'_apply, hπ]
    simp [hw]
  · rintro _ ⟨x, rfl⟩ v rfl
    simp

/-- For injective coefficients, the system of equations `x (g x)⁻¹`, `x ∈ X`, whose only solution
is the point `ι ∘ g`, is radical: its radical is its normal closure. -/
theorem Rad_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : X → G) :
    Rad ι (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹) =
      normalClosure (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹) := by
  rw [Rad_def, ← singleton_comp_eq_V, I_singleton_comp hι]

/-- For injective coefficients, the radical of the one-variable equation `x * b = 1` is its
normal closure: the quotient `G[x] ⧸ ncl(x b)` is `G` itself. -/
theorem Rad_inr_of_mul_inl [Subsingleton X] (hι : Function.Injective ι) (x : X) (b : G) :
    Rad ι {(inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) * inl b} =
      normalClosure {inr (FreeGroup.of x) * inl b} := by
  have := uniqueOfSubsingleton x
  simpa [Set.range_unique, Subsingleton.elim default x] using
    Rad_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl hι fun _ : X => b⁻¹

/-- For injective coefficients, the radical of the one-variable equation `x = 1` is its normal
closure. -/
theorem Rad_singleton_inr_of [Subsingleton X] (hι : Function.Injective ι) (x : X) :
    Rad ι {(inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)} = normalClosure {inr (FreeGroup.of x)} := by
  simpa using Rad_inr_of_mul_inl hι x 1

/-! ### Quotients of `G[X]` -/

namespace GX

variable (N : Subgroup (GX G X)) [N.Normal]

/-- The coefficient homomorphism `G →* G[X] ⧸ N` of a quotient of `G[X]`. -/
def quotientCoeff : G →* GX G X ⧸ N :=
  (QuotientGroup.mk' N).comp inl

theorem quotientCoeff_def : quotientCoeff N = (QuotientGroup.mk' N).comp inl := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem quotientCoeff_apply (g : G) : quotientCoeff N g = ((inl g : GX G X) : GX G X ⧸ N) := (rfl)

variable {N}

/-- A `G`-homomorphism `G[X] ⧸ N → H`, composed with the quotient map, restricts to `ι` on the
coefficients. -/
theorem comp_mk'_comp_inl (φ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι) :
    ((φ : GX G X ⧸ N →* H).comp (QuotientGroup.mk' N)).comp inl = ι := by
  rw [MonoidHom.comp_assoc, ← quotientCoeff_def, φ.comp_coeff]

/-- A `G`-homomorphism `G[X] ⧸ N → H`, composed with the quotient map, is evaluation at its values
on the variables. -/
theorem eval_eq_comp_mk' (φ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι) :
    eval ι (fun x => φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)) =
      (φ : GX G X ⧸ N →* H).comp (QuotientGroup.mk' N) := by
  simpa using eval_eq_of_comp_inl (comp_mk'_comp_inl φ)

variable (v) in
/-- Evaluation at a point `v` at which every equation of `N` vanishes, as a `G`-homomorphism
`G[X] ⧸ N → H`. -/
def quotientEvalGHom (hv : v ∈ V ι (N : Set (GX G X))) : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι where
  toMonoidHom := QuotientGroup.lift N (eval ι v) fun w hw => mem_V.1 hv w hw
  map_coeff' := eval_inl ι v

@[simp]
theorem quotientEvalGHom_mk (hv : v ∈ V ι (N : Set (GX G X))) (w : GX G X) :
    quotientEvalGHom v hv (w : GX G X ⧸ N) = eval ι v w := (rfl)

variable (ι N) in
/-- The universal property of a quotient `G[X] ⧸ N`: `G`-homomorphisms `G[X] ⧸ N → H` correspond
to the points of `H^X` at which every equation of `N` vanishes, by restriction to the variables and
by evaluation. -/
def quotientHomEquiv : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι ≃ V ι (N : Set (GX G X)) where
  toFun φ := ⟨fun x => φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X), mem_V.2 fun w hw => by
    rw [eval_eq_comp_mk', MonoidHom.comp_apply, QuotientGroup.mk'_apply,
      (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff w).2 hw, map_one]⟩
  invFun v := quotientEvalGHom v.1 v.2
  left_inv φ := by
    refine GHom.ext fun q => ?_
    obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk_surjective q
    exact DFunLike.congr_fun (eval_eq_comp_mk' φ) w
  right_inv v := Subtype.ext (funext fun x => eval_inr_of ι v.1 x)

@[simp]
theorem quotientHomEquiv_apply_coe (φ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι) (x : X) :
    (quotientHomEquiv ι N φ : X → H) x = φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) := (rfl)

/-- Evaluation at the point corresponding to a `G`-homomorphism `φ : G[X] ⧸ N → H` is `φ`
composed with the quotient map. -/
theorem eval_quotientHomEquiv (φ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι) (w : GX G X) :
    eval ι (quotientHomEquiv ι N φ) w = φ (w : GX G X ⧸ N) :=
  DFunLike.congr_fun (eval_eq_comp_mk' φ) w

@[simp]
theorem quotientHomEquiv_symm_apply (v : V ι (N : Set (GX G X))) :
    (quotientHomEquiv ι N).symm v = quotientEvalGHom v v.2 := (rfl)

/-- A `G`-homomorphism out of `G[X] ⧸ N` is determined by its values on the variables. -/
@[ext]
theorem quotientGHom_ext {φ ψ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) ι}
    (h : ∀ x, φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) = ψ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)) : φ = ψ :=
  (quotientHomEquiv ι N).injective (Subtype.ext (funext h))

/-- If `ι` is injective and the equations of `N` have a common zero in `H`, the coefficients
embed in `G[X] ⧸ N`. -/
theorem quotientCoeff_injective (hι : Function.Injective ι)
    (hN : (V ι (N : Set (GX G X))).Nonempty) : Function.Injective (quotientCoeff N) := by
  obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := hN
  refine Function.Injective.of_comp (f := quotientEvalGHom v hv) ?_
  convert hι using 1
  ext g
  simp

/-- A surjective `G`-homomorphism `π : G[X] →* K` induces a `G`-isomorphism `G[X] ⧸ N → K` from
the quotient by its kernel `N`. -/
theorem exists_bijective_gHom_of_eq_ker {K : Type*} [Group K] {κ : G →* K}
    (π : GHom (inl : G →* GX G X) κ) (hπ : Function.Surjective π)
    (hN : N = (π : GX G X →* K).ker) :
    ∃ ψ : GHom (quotientCoeff N) κ, Function.Bijective ψ ∧
      ∀ w : GX G X, ψ (w : GX G X ⧸ N) = π w := by
  let e := QuotientGroup.liftEquiv N (φ := (π : GX G X →* K)) hπ hN
  refine ⟨⟨e.toMonoidHom, fun g => ?_⟩, e.bijective, fun w => rfl⟩
  simp [e]

/-- Let `K` be presented over `G` by a surjective `G`-homomorphism `π : G[X] →* K`. A point
`v ∈ H^X` at which every relation of `K` vanishes defines a `G`-homomorphism `K → H` through which
evaluation at `v` factors. -/
theorem exists_gHom_apply_eq_eval {K : Type*} [Group K] {κ : G →* K}
    {π : GHom (inl : G →* GX G X) κ} (hπ : Function.Surjective π)
    (hv : v ∈ V ι ((π : GX G X →* K).ker : Set (GX G X))) :
    ∃ φ : GHom κ ι, ∀ w, φ (π w) = eval ι v w := by
  obtain ⟨ψ, hψ, hψπ⟩ := exists_bijective_gHom_of_eq_ker π hπ rfl
  exact ⟨(quotientEvalGHom v hv).comp (ψ.symmOfBijective hψ), fun w => by simp [← hψπ]⟩

end GX

/-! ### Coordinate groups and the group of a system -/

variable (ι) in
/-- The coordinate group `G_{R(S)} = G[X] ⧸ Rad_H(S)` of a system `S` over `H`. -/
abbrev CoordGroup (S : Set (GX G X)) : Type _ :=
  GX G X ⧸ Rad ι S

namespace CoordGroup

variable (ι) in
/-- The coefficient homomorphism `G →* G_{R(S)}`. -/
def coeff (S : Set (GX G X)) : G →* CoordGroup ι S :=
  GX.quotientCoeff _

theorem coeff_def (S : Set (GX G X)) : coeff ι S = GX.quotientCoeff (Rad ι S) := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem coeff_apply (S : Set (GX G X)) (g : G) :
    coeff ι S g = ((inl g : GX G X) : CoordGroup ι S) := (rfl)

variable (v) in
/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` of `S` in `H`, as a `G`-homomorphism `G_{R(S)} → H`. -/
def evalGHom (hv : v ∈ V ι S) : GHom (coeff ι S) ι :=
  GX.quotientEvalGHom v ((V_Rad (ι := ι) S).ge hv)

@[simp]
theorem evalGHom_mk (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (w : GX G X) :
    evalGHom v hv (w : CoordGroup ι S) = eval ι v w := (rfl)

variable (ι S) in
/-- The universal property of the coordinate group: `G`-homomorphisms `G_{R(S)} → H` correspond
to the solutions of `S` in `H`, by restriction to the variables and by evaluation. -/
def homEquiv : GHom (coeff ι S) ι ≃ V ι S :=
  (GX.quotientHomEquiv ι _).trans (Set.equivOfEq (V_Rad S))

@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_apply_coe (φ : GHom (coeff ι S) ι) (x : X) :
    (homEquiv ι S φ : X → H) x = φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_symm_apply (v : V ι S) : (homEquiv ι S).symm v = evalGHom v v.2 := (rfl)

/-- Evaluation at a solution agrees with the corresponding coordinate-group homomorphism. -/
@[simp]
theorem eval_homEquiv (S : Set (GX G X)) (φ : GHom (coeff ι S) ι) (w : GX G X) :
    eval ι (homEquiv ι S φ) w = φ (w : CoordGroup ι S) :=
  GX.eval_quotientHomEquiv φ w

/-- A `G`-homomorphism out of `G_{R(S)}` is determined by its values on the variables. -/
@[ext]
theorem gHom_ext {K : Type*} [Group K] {κ : G →* K}
    {φ ψ : GHom (coeff ι S) κ}
    (h : ∀ x, φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) = ψ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)) : φ = ψ :=
  GX.quotientGHom_ext h

/-- If `ι` is injective and the system `S` has a solution in `H`, the coefficients embed in the
coordinate group `G_{R(S)}`. -/
theorem coeff_injective (hι : Function.Injective ι) (hS : (V ι S).Nonempty) :
    Function.Injective (coeff ι S) :=
  GX.quotientCoeff_injective hι (hS.mono (V_Rad (ι := ι) S).ge)

variable (ι) in
/-- The coordinate group `G_{R(S)}` of a system with a solution in a `G`-group `H`, as a
`G`-group. -/
@[expose] def toGGroup (hι : Function.Injective ι) (hS : (V ι S).Nonempty) : GGroup G :=
  ⟨CoordGroup ι S, coeff ι S, coeff_injective hι hS⟩

@[simp]
theorem toGGroup_coeff (hι : Function.Injective ι) (hS : (V ι S).Nonempty) :
    (toGGroup ι hι hS).coeff = coeff ι S := (rfl)

/-- The coordinate group of `S` is trivial if and only if evaluation at every solution of `S` in
`H` is trivial. -/
theorem subsingleton_iff : Subsingleton (CoordGroup ι S) ↔ ∀ v ∈ V ι S, eval ι v = 1 :=
  QuotientGroup.subsingleton_iff.trans Rad_eq_top_iff

/-- The coordinate group of a system without solutions in `H` is trivial. -/
theorem subsingleton_of_V_eq_empty (hS : V ι S = ∅) : Subsingleton (CoordGroup ι S) :=
  QuotientGroup.subsingleton_iff.2 (Rad_eq_top_of_V_eq_empty hS)

/-- Every coordinate group over a trivial group is trivial. -/
instance [Subsingleton H] : Subsingleton (CoordGroup ι S) :=
  subsingleton_iff.2 fun _ _ => MonoidHom.ext fun _ => Subsingleton.elim _ _

variable (ι) in
/-- The generic point `μ : X → G_{R(S)}` of a system `S`: the tuple of the images of the
variables in the coordinate group. -/
def genericPoint (S : Set (GX G X)) : X → CoordGroup ι S :=
  fun x => ((inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) : CoordGroup ι S)

theorem genericPoint_apply (S : Set (GX G X)) (x : X) :
    genericPoint ι S x = ((inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) : CoordGroup ι S) := (rfl)

/-- Evaluating an equation at the generic point gives its class in the coordinate group. -/
@[simp]
theorem eval_genericPoint (S : Set (GX G X)) (w : GX G X) :
    eval (coeff ι S) (genericPoint ι S) w = (w : CoordGroup ι S) :=
  DFunLike.congr_fun (eval_eq_of_comp_inl (f := QuotientGroup.mk' (Rad ι S)) rfl) w

/-- The generic point of `S` is a solution of `S` in the coordinate group. -/
theorem genericPoint_mem_V (S : Set (GX G X)) : genericPoint ι S ∈ V (coeff ι S) S :=
  mem_V.2 fun s hs => by
    rw [eval_genericPoint]
    exact (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff s).2 (subset_Rad S hs)

/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` sends each coordinate of the generic point to that of `v`. -/
@[simp]
theorem evalGHom_genericPoint (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (x : X) :
    evalGHom v hv (genericPoint ι S x) = v x :=
  eval_inr_of ι v x

/-- Composing the generic point with evaluation at a solution `v` gives `v`. -/
@[simp]
theorem evalGHom_comp_genericPoint (hv : v ∈ V ι S) : evalGHom v hv ∘ genericPoint ι S = v :=
  funext (evalGHom_genericPoint hv)

/-- If every solution of `S` is the point with coordinates `ι (g x)`, the generic point of `S` has
the coefficients `g x` as coordinates. -/
theorem genericPoint_eq_coeff_comp {g : X → G} (hS : V ι S ⊆ {ι ∘ g}) :
    genericPoint ι S = coeff ι S ∘ g := by
  funext x
  rw [genericPoint_apply, Function.comp_apply, coeff_apply, QuotientGroup.eq]
  refine mem_Rad.2 fun v hv => ?_
  obtain rfl := hS hv
  simp

/-- A coordinate of the generic point of `S` is trivial if and only if it is trivial at every
solution of `S`. -/
theorem genericPoint_eq_one_iff {x : X} : genericPoint ι S x = 1 ↔ ∀ v ∈ V ι S, v x = 1 := by
  simp [genericPoint_apply, QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff, mem_Rad]

/-- A coordinate of the generic point of `S` is nontrivial if and only if it is nontrivial at
some solution of `S`. -/
theorem genericPoint_ne_one_iff {x : X} : genericPoint ι S x ≠ 1 ↔ ∃ v ∈ V ι S, v x ≠ 1 := by
  simp [genericPoint_eq_one_iff]

/-- The coordinate group of a system without solutions in `H` is not a `G`-group: its coefficient
homomorphism is not injective. -/
theorem not_injective_coeff_of_V_eq_empty (hS : V ι S = ∅) :
    ¬Function.Injective (coeff ι S) := by
  intro h
  -- The radical is everything, so all coefficients become trivial and `G` must be trivial.
  have hG : ∀ g : G, g = 1 := fun g => h <| by simp [hS]
  -- Over trivial coefficients the constant point `1` solves every equation.
  have h1 : eval ι (1 : X → H) = 1 :=
    hom_ext (MonoidHom.ext fun g => by simp [hG g]) (FreeGroup.ext_hom _ _ fun x => by simp)
  exact hS.subset ((mem_V (v := 1)).2 fun s _ => by simp [h1])

/-- The coordinate group of `S` is a `G`-group, that is, its coefficient homomorphism is
injective, exactly when `ι` is injective and `S` has a solution in `H`. In particular, over a
`G`-group `H` the coordinate groups of nonempty algebraic sets are `G`-groups and that of the empty
set is not. -/
theorem coeff_injective_iff :
    Function.Injective (coeff ι S) ↔ Function.Injective ι ∧ (V ι S).Nonempty := by
  refine ⟨fun h => ⟨fun g g' hg => h ?_, ?_⟩, fun h => coeff_injective h.1 h.2⟩
  · -- Coefficients with the same image in `H` agree at every point, so they agree in `G_{R(S)}`.
    exact QuotientGroup.eq.2 (mem_Rad.2 fun v _ => by simp [hg])
  · exact Set.nonempty_iff_ne_empty.2 fun hS => not_injective_coeff_of_V_eq_empty hS h

/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` maps the coordinate group onto the subgroup of `H` generated by
the coefficients and the coordinates of `v`. -/
theorem range_evalGHom (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    (evalGHom v hv : CoordGroup ι S →* H).range = ι.range ⊔ closure (Set.range v) := by
  -- The quotient map is surjective, so the range is that of evaluation on `G[X]`.
  have h : (evalGHom v hv : CoordGroup ι S →* H).comp (QuotientGroup.mk' _) = eval ι v :=
    MonoidHom.ext (evalGHom_mk hv)
  rw [MonoidHom.range_eq_map, ← MonoidHom.range_eq_top.2 (QuotientGroup.mk'_surjective (Rad ι S)),
    MonoidHom.map_range, h, Monoid.Coprod.range_eq, eval_comp_inl, eval_comp_inr,
    FreeGroup.range_lift_eq_closure]

/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` is injective on the coordinate group exactly when every equation
vanishing at `v` vanishes on all of `V_H(S)`, that is, when `v` is a generic point of `V_H(S)`. -/
theorem evalGHom_injective_iff (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    Function.Injective (evalGHom v hv) ↔ I ι {v} ≤ Rad ι S := by
  have hle : Rad ι S ≤ (eval ι v).ker := fun w hw => MonoidHom.mem_ker.2 (mem_Rad.1 hw v hv)
  have hI : I ι {v} = (eval ι v).ker := by simp [I_eq_iInf_ker]
  rw [hI]
  exact (QuotientGroup.injective_lift_iff _ _ hle).trans (le_antisymm_iff.trans (and_iff_right hle))

/-- If `v` is the only solution of `S`, evaluation at `v` is injective on the coordinate group.
Together with `range_evalGHom`, this identifies the coordinate group of the point `v` with the
subgroup of `H` generated by the coefficients and the coordinates of `v`. -/
theorem evalGHom_injective (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (hS : V ι S ⊆ {v}) :
    Function.Injective (evalGHom v hv) :=
  (evalGHom_injective_iff hv).2 (I_antitone ι hS)

/-- If `v` is the only solution of `S`, every coefficient-preserving homomorphism `G_{R(S)} → H`
is evaluation at `v`. -/
theorem eq_evalGHom (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (hS : V ι S ⊆ {v}) (φ : GHom (coeff ι S) ι) :
    φ = evalGHom v hv := by
  refine (homEquiv ι S).injective (Subtype.ext ((hS (homEquiv ι S φ).2).trans ?_))
  funext x
  simp

variable {Y : Type*}
variable (ι : G →* H) (S : Set (GX G X)) (T : Set (GX G Y))

/-- A tuple of words maps the solution set of `S` into the solution set of `T`. -/
def MapsToWordMap (w : Y → GX G X) : Prop :=
  Set.MapsTo (wordMap ι w) (V ι S) (V ι T)

/-- A tuple of words maps solutions of `S` to solutions of `T` precisely when its word map
does so at every solution. -/
theorem mapsToWordMap_iff (w : Y → GX G X) :
    MapsToWordMap ι S T w ↔ ∀ v ∈ V ι S, wordMap ι w v ∈ V ι T :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- A word map between algebraic sets pulls equations vanishing on the target back to
equations vanishing on the source. -/
theorem Rad_le_comap_of_mapsToWordMap (w : Y → GX G X) (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w) :
    Rad ι T ≤ (Rad ι S).comap (eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w) := by
  intro t ht
  rw [Subgroup.mem_comap, mem_Rad]
  intro v hv
  rw [← MonoidHom.comp_apply, eval_comp_eval]
  exact (mem_Rad.mp ht) _ ((mapsToWordMap_iff ι S T w).mp hw v hv)

/-- The tuple of variables maps an algebraic set to itself. -/
theorem mapsToWordMap_id : MapsToWordMap ι S S fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) :=
  (mapsToWordMap_iff ι S S _).2 fun v hv => by rwa [wordMap_id]

variable {ι S T} in
/-- Substituting one tuple of words into another composes the corresponding maps of algebraic
sets. -/
theorem MapsToWordMap.comp {Z : Type*} {U : Set (GX G Z)} {w : Y → GX G X} {u : Z → GX G Y}
    (hu : MapsToWordMap ι T U u) (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w) :
    MapsToWordMap ι S U fun z => eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w (u z) := by
  refine (mapsToWordMap_iff ι S U _).2 fun v hv => ?_
  rw [← wordMap_wordMap]
  exact (mapsToWordMap_iff ι T U u).1 hu _ ((mapsToWordMap_iff ι S T w).1 hw v hv)

/-- A word map between algebraic sets induces a coefficient-preserving homomorphism of
coordinate groups in the opposite direction. -/
def homOfMapsToWordMap (w : Y → GX G X) (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w) :
    GHom (coeff ι T) (coeff ι S) where
  toMonoidHom := QuotientGroup.map _ _ (eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w)
    (Rad_le_comap_of_mapsToWordMap ι S T w hw)
  map_coeff' g := by simp [coeff_apply]

@[simp]
theorem homOfMapsToWordMap_mk (w : Y → GX G X) (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w)
    (t : GX G Y) :
    homOfMapsToWordMap ι S T w hw (t : CoordGroup ι T) =
      (eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w t : CoordGroup ι S) := by
  simp [homOfMapsToWordMap]

/-- The tuple of variables induces the identity of the coordinate group. -/
@[simp]
theorem homOfMapsToWordMap_id :
    homOfMapsToWordMap ι S S (fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x)) (mapsToWordMap_id ι S) =
      GHom.id (coeff ι S) :=
  gHom_ext fun _ => by simp

/-- The homomorphism induced by a composite of word maps is the composite of the induced
homomorphisms, in the opposite order. -/
theorem homOfMapsToWordMap_comp {Z : Type*} (U : Set (GX G Z)) {w : Y → GX G X}
    {u : Z → GX G Y} (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w) (hu : MapsToWordMap ι T U u) :
    homOfMapsToWordMap ι S U (fun z => eval (inl : G →* GX G X) w (u z)) (hu.comp hw) =
      (homOfMapsToWordMap ι S T w hw).comp (homOfMapsToWordMap ι T U u hu) :=
  gHom_ext fun _ => by simp

/-- Evaluating the induced coordinate-group homomorphism at a point is the original word
map of that point. -/
theorem homEquiv_comp_homOfMapsToWordMap (w : Y → GX G X)
    (hw : MapsToWordMap ι S T w) (v : V ι S) :
    (homEquiv ι T ((evalGHom v.1 v.2).comp
      (homOfMapsToWordMap ι S T w hw)) : Y → H) = wordMap ι w v.1 := by
  funext y
  simp only [homEquiv_apply_coe, GHom.coe_comp, Function.comp_apply,
    homOfMapsToWordMap_mk, evalGHom_mk, wordMap_apply, eval_inr_of]

/-- A coefficient-preserving word map induces a homomorphism between coordinate groups. -/
def homOfWordMap {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (S : Set (GX G X)) : GHom (coeff ι (σ ⁻¹' S)) (coeff ι S) :=
  homOfMapsToWordMap ι S (σ ⁻¹' S) (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y)))
    (fun v hv => wordMap_mem_V ι σ S v hv)

@[simp]
theorem homOfWordMap_mk {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (S : Set (GX G X)) (w : GX G Y) :
    homOfWordMap ι σ S (w : CoordGroup ι (σ ⁻¹' S)) =
      (σ w : CoordGroup ι S) := by
  simp only [homOfWordMap, homOfMapsToWordMap]
  exact congrArg (fun t : GX G X => (t : CoordGroup ι S))
    (DFunLike.congr_fun (eval_eq_of_comp_inl σ.comp_coeff) w)

/-- Precomposition with the coordinate-group word map corresponds to the word map on
tuples under the point–homomorphism equivalence. -/
theorem homEquiv_comp_homOfWordMap {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X))
    (S : Set (GX G X)) (φ : GHom (coeff ι S) ι) :
    ((homEquiv ι (σ ⁻¹' S) (φ.comp (homOfWordMap ι σ S)) : Y → H)) =
      wordMap ι (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) (homEquiv ι S φ) := by
  funext y; simp

end CoordGroup

/-- The word homomorphism `σ` maps the radical of the inverse-image system into the
radical of the original system. -/
theorem Rad_preimage_le_comap {Y : Type*} (ι : G →* H)
    (σ : GHom (inl : G →* GX G Y) (inl : G →* GX G X)) (S : Set (GX G X)) :
    Rad ι (σ ⁻¹' S) ≤ (Rad ι S).comap σ.toMonoidHom := by
  have h := CoordGroup.Rad_le_comap_of_mapsToWordMap ι S (σ ⁻¹' S)
    (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) (fun v hv => wordMap_mem_V ι σ S v hv)
  have heval : eval (inl : G →* GX G X) (fun y => σ (inr (FreeGroup.of y))) =
      σ.toMonoidHom := eval_eq_of_comp_inl σ.comp_coeff
  rwa [heval] at h

variable (G) in
/-- The group `G_S = G[X] ⧸ ncl(S)` presented over `G` by the system `S` of equations. -/
abbrev SystemGroup (S : Set (GX G X)) : Type _ :=
  GX G X ⧸ normalClosure S

namespace SystemGroup

/-- The coefficient homomorphism `G →* G_S`. -/
def coeff (S : Set (GX G X)) : G →* SystemGroup G S :=
  GX.quotientCoeff _

theorem coeff_def (S : Set (GX G X)) : coeff S = GX.quotientCoeff (normalClosure S) := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem coeff_apply (S : Set (GX G X)) (g : G) :
    coeff S g = ((inl g : GX G X) : SystemGroup G S) := (rfl)

variable (ι) in
/-- The canonical surjection `G_S →* G_{R(S)}`, a `G`-homomorphism. -/
def toCoordGroup (S : Set (GX G X)) : GHom (coeff S) (CoordGroup.coeff ι S) where
  toMonoidHom := QuotientGroup.map _ _ (.id _) (by simpa using normalClosure_le_Rad S)
  map_coeff' _ := (rfl)

variable (v) in
/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` of `S` in `H`, as a `G`-homomorphism `G_S → H`. -/
def evalGHom (hv : v ∈ V ι S) : GHom (coeff S) ι :=
  GX.quotientEvalGHom v ((V_normalClosure (ι := ι) S).ge hv)

@[simp]
theorem evalGHom_mk (hv : v ∈ V ι S) (w : GX G X) :
    evalGHom v hv (w : SystemGroup G S) = eval ι v w := (rfl)

variable (ι S) in
/-- The universal property of `G_S`: `G`-homomorphisms `G_S → H` correspond to the solutions of
`S` in `H`, by restriction to the variables and by evaluation. -/
def homEquiv : GHom (coeff S) ι ≃ V ι S :=
  (GX.quotientHomEquiv ι _).trans (Set.equivOfEq (V_normalClosure S))

@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_apply_coe (φ : GHom (coeff S) ι) (x : X) :
    (homEquiv ι S φ : X → H) x = φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) := (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_symm_apply (v : V ι S) : (homEquiv ι S).symm v = evalGHom v v.2 := (rfl)

/-- A `G`-homomorphism out of `G_S` is determined by its values on the variables. -/
@[ext]
theorem gHom_ext {φ ψ : GHom (coeff S) ι}
    (h : ∀ x, φ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X) = ψ (inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)) : φ = ψ :=
  GX.quotientGHom_ext h

@[simp]
theorem toCoordGroup_mk (S : Set (GX G X)) (w : GX G X) :
    toCoordGroup ι S (w : SystemGroup G S) = (w : CoordGroup ι S) := (rfl)

theorem toCoordGroup_surjective (S : Set (GX G X)) :
    Function.Surjective (toCoordGroup ι S) := by
  intro q
  obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk_surjective q
  exact ⟨w, toCoordGroup_mk S w⟩

/-- If `ι` is injective and the system `S` has a solution in `H`, the coefficients embed in
`G_S`. -/
theorem coeff_injective (hι : Function.Injective ι) (hS : (V ι S).Nonempty) :
    Function.Injective (coeff S) :=
  GX.quotientCoeff_injective hι (hS.mono (V_normalClosure (ι := ι) S).ge)

variable (ι) in
/-- The group `G_S` has no `G`-homomorphism to `H` exactly when `S` has no solution in `H`. -/
theorem isEmpty_gHom_iff (S : Set (GX G X)) : IsEmpty (GHom (coeff S) ι) ↔ V ι S = ∅ :=
  (homEquiv ι S).isEmpty_congr.trans Set.isEmpty_coe_sort

/-- A constant occurring as an equation of `S` becomes trivial in `G_S`. -/
theorem coeff_eq_one_of_inl_mem {g : G} (hS : inl g ∈ S) : coeff S g = 1 :=
  (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff _).2 (subset_normalClosure hS)

/-- If a nontrivial constant occurs as an equation of `S`, the coefficients do not embed in
`G_S`. -/
theorem not_injective_coeff_of_inl_mem {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 1) (hS : inl g ∈ S) :
    ¬Function.Injective (coeff S) := fun h =>
  hg (h ((coeff_eq_one_of_inl_mem hS).trans (map_one _).symm))

/-- The group of the system `x (g x)⁻¹`, `x ∈ X`, which prescribes the value `g x` of every
variable, is `G` itself: its coefficient homomorphism is bijective. -/
theorem coeff_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl_bijective (g : X → G) :
    Function.Bijective (coeff (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹)) := by
  -- The normal closure of the system is the kernel of evaluation at `g`, a retraction onto `G`.
  have hN : normalClosure (Set.range fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x) * (inl (g x))⁻¹) =
      (GroupAlgGeom.evalGHom (.id G) g : GX G X →* G).ker := by
    rw [← I_singleton_comp (ι := .id G) Function.injective_id]
    ext w
    simp
  obtain ⟨ψ, hψ, -⟩ := GX.exists_bijective_gHom_of_eq_ker (GroupAlgGeom.evalGHom (.id G) g)
    (fun a => ⟨inl a, by simp⟩) hN
  refine (Function.Bijective.of_comp_iff' hψ _).1 ?_
  convert Function.bijective_id using 1
  exact funext ψ.map_coeff

/-- The group of the one-variable equation `x = 1` is `G` itself: its coefficient homomorphism
is bijective. -/
theorem coeff_singleton_inr_of_bijective [Subsingleton X] (x : X) :
    Function.Bijective (coeff {(inr (FreeGroup.of x) : GX G X)}) := by
  have := uniqueOfSubsingleton x
  have h := coeff_range_inr_of_mul_inv_inl_bijective fun _ : X => (1 : G)
  rwa [Set.range_unique, Subsingleton.elim default x, map_one, inv_one, mul_one] at h

/-- Restricting a `G`-homomorphism `G_{R(S)} → H` along `G_S → G_{R(S)}` gives the same solution
of `S`. -/
@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_comp_toCoordGroup (φ : GHom (CoordGroup.coeff ι S) ι) :
    homEquiv ι S (φ.comp (toCoordGroup ι S)) = CoordGroup.homEquiv ι S φ :=
  Subtype.ext (funext fun _ => by simp)

end SystemGroup

/-- Evaluation at a solution `v` on `G_S` factors through `G_S → G_{R(S)}`. -/
@[simp]
theorem CoordGroup.evalGHom_comp_toCoordGroup (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    (evalGHom v hv).comp (SystemGroup.toCoordGroup ι S) = SystemGroup.evalGHom v hv := by
  ext
  simp

/-! ### Systems in the same variables with nested solution sets -/

namespace CoordGroup

/-- If every solution of `S` in `H` is a solution of `T`, the identity on the variables induces a
`G`-homomorphism `G_{R(T)} → G_{R(S)}` of coordinate groups. -/
def homOfVSubset (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) : GHom (coeff ι T) (coeff ι S) :=
  homOfMapsToWordMap ι S T (fun x => inr (FreeGroup.of x)) <|
    (mapsToWordMap_iff ι S T _).2 fun v hv => by simpa only [wordMap_id] using h hv

@[simp]
theorem homOfVSubset_mk (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) (w : GX G X) :
    homOfVSubset h (w : CoordGroup ι T) = (w : CoordGroup ι S) := by
  rw [homOfVSubset, homOfMapsToWordMap_mk, eval_inl_inr_of, MonoidHom.id_apply]

/-- The homomorphism induced by the identity on the variables is surjective. -/
theorem homOfVSubset_surjective (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) : Function.Surjective (homOfVSubset h) := by
  intro q
  obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk_surjective q
  exact ⟨w, homOfVSubset_mk h w⟩

/-- When `S` and `T` have the same solutions in `H`, the homomorphism induced by the identity on
the variables is injective, so it is an isomorphism `G_{R(T)} ≅ G_{R(S)}`. -/
theorem homOfVSubset_injective (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) (h' : V ι T ⊆ V ι S) :
    Function.Injective (homOfVSubset h) := by
  refine (injective_iff_map_eq_one _).2 fun q hq => ?_
  obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := QuotientGroup.mk_surjective q
  rw [homOfVSubset_mk, QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff, mem_Rad] at hq
  exact (QuotientGroup.eq_one_iff w).2 (mem_Rad.2 fun v hv => hq v (h' hv))

/-- A system compared with itself gives the identity homomorphism. -/
@[simp]
theorem homOfVSubset_refl : homOfVSubset (subset_refl (V ι S)) = GHom.id (coeff ι S) := by
  ext
  simp

/-- The homomorphisms induced by the identity on the variables compose, in the opposite order. -/
@[simp]
theorem homOfVSubset_comp {U : Set (GX G X)} (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) (h' : V ι T ⊆ V ι U) :
    (homOfVSubset h).comp (homOfVSubset h') = homOfVSubset (h.trans h') := by
  ext
  simp

/-- A solution of `S` given by a `G`-homomorphism `G_{R(S)} → H` is the same solution of `T`. -/
@[simp]
theorem homEquiv_comp_homOfVSubset (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) (φ : GHom (coeff ι S) ι) :
    (homEquiv ι T (φ.comp (homOfVSubset h)) : X → H) = homEquiv ι S φ := by
  ext
  simp

/-- Evaluation at a solution of `S` factors through the homomorphism `G_{R(T)} → G_{R(S)}`
induced by the identity on the variables. -/
@[simp]
theorem evalGHom_comp_homOfVSubset (h : V ι S ⊆ V ι T) (hv : v ∈ V ι S) :
    (evalGHom v hv).comp (homOfVSubset h) = evalGHom v (h hv) := by
  ext
  simp

end CoordGroup

/-! ### The radical is not the normal closure -/

section Pow

open FreeGroup

/-- Over a torsion-free group `H` with injective coefficients, the radical of the one-variable
equation `x ^ n = 1` (`n ≠ 0`) is the normal closure of `x`. -/
theorem Rad_pow_eq_normalClosure [IsMulTorsionFree H] (hι : Function.Injective ι) {n : ℕ}
    (hn : n ≠ 0) :
    Rad ι {(inr (of ()) : GX G Unit) ^ n} = normalClosure {inr (of ())} := by
  have hV : V ι {(inr (of ()) : GX G Unit) ^ n} = {ι ∘ fun _ => 1} := by
    ext v
    simp [hn, funext_iff, Unique.forall_iff]
  rw [Rad_def, hV, I_singleton_comp hι]
  simp [Set.range_unique]

/-- In a group `H` with unique roots, the equation `x ^ n * b ^ n = 1` (`n ≠ 0`) has the same
solutions as `x * b = 1`: the only `n`-th root of `(b ^ n)⁻¹` is `b⁻¹`. -/
theorem V_pow_mul_inl_pow [HasUniqueRoots H] {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) (x : X) (b : G) :
    V ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n * inl (b ^ n)} = V ι {inr (of x) * inl b} := by
  ext v
  simp [mul_eq_one_iff_eq_inv, ← inv_pow, pow_left_inj hn]

/-- In a group `H` with unique roots, the equations `x ^ n * b ^ n = 1` (`n ≠ 0`) and `x * b = 1`
have the same radical. -/
theorem Rad_pow_mul_inl_pow [HasUniqueRoots H] {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) (x : X) (b : G) :
    Rad ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n * inl (b ^ n)} = Rad ι {inr (of x) * inl b} := by
  rw [Rad_def, V_pow_mul_inl_pow hn, Rad_def]

/-- Over a group `H` with unique roots and injective coefficients, the radical of the
one-variable equation `x ^ n * b ^ n = 1` (`n ≠ 0`) is the normal closure of `x * b`. -/
theorem Rad_pow_mul_inl_pow_eq_normalClosure [Subsingleton X] [HasUniqueRoots H]
    (hι : Function.Injective ι) {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 0) (x : X) (b : G) :
    Rad ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n * inl (b ^ n)} = normalClosure {inr (of x) * inl b} := by
  rw [Rad_pow_mul_inl_pow hn, Rad_inr_of_mul_inl hι]

/-- When `n ≠ 1`, the normal closure of the equation `x ^ n * d` does not contain `x * b`, for
any coefficients `b` and `d`. -/
theorem inr_of_mul_inl_notMem_normalClosure_pow_mul_inl {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 1) (x : X) (b d : G) :
    (inr (of x) * inl b : GX G X) ∉ normalClosure {inr (of x) ^ n * inl d} := by
  -- Send the coefficients to `1` and the variables to a generator of `ℤ / n`.
  let f : GX G X →* Multiplicative (ZMod n) := eval 1 fun _ => .ofAdd 1
  have hle : normalClosure {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n * inl d} ≤ f.ker := by
    refine normalClosure_le_normal ?_
    rintro _ rfl
    simp [f, ← ofAdd_nsmul]
  intro h
  have : (Multiplicative.ofAdd 1 : Multiplicative (ZMod n)) = 1 := by simpa [f] using hle h
  exact hn (ZMod.one_eq_zero_iff.1 this)

/-- The normal closure of the one-variable equation `x ^ n` does not contain `x` when `n ≠ 1`. -/
theorem inr_of_notMem_normalClosure_pow {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 1) (x : X) :
    (inr (of x) : GX G X) ∉ normalClosure {inr (of x) ^ n} := by
  simpa using inr_of_mul_inl_notMem_normalClosure_pow_mul_inl hn x (1 : G) 1

/-- For `n ≠ 1`, the normal closure of `x ^ n` differs from the normal closure of `x`. -/
theorem normalClosure_pow_ne_normalClosure {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≠ 1) (x : X) :
    normalClosure {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n} ≠ normalClosure {inr (of x)} := fun h =>
  inr_of_notMem_normalClosure_pow hn x (by rw [h]; exact subset_normalClosure rfl)

/-- Over `G = F(a, b)` with one variable `x`, the radical of `x²` is the normal closure of `x`,
which is not the normal closure of `x²`. -/
theorem rad_sq :
    Rad (.id (FreeGroup (Fin 2))) {(inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit) ^ 2} =
        normalClosure {inr (of ())} ∧
      normalClosure {(inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit) ^ 2} ≠
        normalClosure {inr (of ())} :=
  ⟨Rad_pow_eq_normalClosure Function.injective_id two_ne_zero,
    normalClosure_pow_ne_normalClosure (by decide) ()⟩

end Pow

/-! ### Equations of exponent type over a group with torsion -/

section Torsion

open FreeGroup

/-- The solutions of the equation `x ^ n = 1` in the variable `x` are the points whose
`x`-coordinate has `n`-th power `1`. -/
theorem V_singleton_inr_of_pow (x : X) (n : ℕ) :
    V ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n} = {v | v x ^ n = 1} := by
  ext
  simp

/-- Every point solves `x ^ n = 1` exactly when every element of `H` has `n`-th power `1`. -/
theorem V_singleton_inr_of_pow_eq_univ_iff (x : X) {n : ℕ} :
    V ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n} = Set.univ ↔ ∀ h : H, h ^ n = 1 := by
  rw [V_singleton_inr_of_pow, Set.eq_univ_iff_forall]
  exact ⟨fun hV h => hV fun _ => h, fun hH v => hH (v x)⟩

/-- Over a group `H` all of whose elements have `n`-th power `1`, the radical of `x ^ n = 1` is
the ideal of all points: the equation carries no information. -/
theorem Rad_pow_eq_I_univ {n : ℕ} (hH : ∀ h : H, h ^ n = 1) (x : X) :
    Rad ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n} = I ι Set.univ := by
  rw [Rad_def, (V_singleton_inr_of_pow_eq_univ_iff x).2 hH]

/-- The variable `x` lies in the radical of `x ^ n = 1` over `H` exactly when `H` has no
nonidentity element of `n`-th power `1`. -/
theorem inr_of_mem_Rad_pow_iff (x : X) {n : ℕ} :
    (inr (of x) : GX G X) ∈ Rad ι {(inr (of x) : GX G X) ^ n} ↔ ∀ h : H, h ^ n = 1 → h = 1 := by
  simp only [mem_Rad, V_singleton_inr_of_pow, Set.mem_ofPred_eq, eval_inr_of]
  exact ⟨fun hV h hn => hV (fun _ => h) hn, fun hH v hn => hH (v x) hn⟩

/-- The radical depends on the group in which solutions are taken: over `G = F(a, b)`, the
variable `x` lies in the radical of `x²` over `G`, but not over `G × ℤ/2`, where `(1, 1)` is a
nontrivial solution of `x² = 1`. -/
theorem rad_sq_prod_zmod_two :
    (inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit) ∈
        Rad (.id (FreeGroup (Fin 2))) {inr (of ()) ^ 2} ∧
      (inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit) ∉
        Rad (.inl (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) (Multiplicative (ZMod 2))) {inr (of ()) ^ 2} := by
  refine ⟨rad_sq.1 ▸ subset_normalClosure rfl, fun h => ?_⟩
  have := (inr_of_mem_Rad_pow_iff ()).1 h (1, .ofAdd 1) (by decide)
  exact absurd this (by decide)

/-- Over `G = ℤ/2` with one variable `x`, every point solves `x² = 1`, so the radical of `x²` is
the ideal of all points. -/
theorem Rad_sq_zmod_two :
    Rad (.id (Multiplicative (ZMod 2))) {(inr (of ()) : GX (Multiplicative (ZMod 2)) Unit) ^ 2} =
      I (.id (Multiplicative (ZMod 2))) Set.univ :=
  Rad_pow_eq_I_univ (by decide) ()

/-- Over `G = ℤ/2` with one variable `x`, the radical of `x²` is not the normal closure of `x`,
in contrast with the torsion-free case. -/
theorem Rad_sq_zmod_two_ne_normalClosure :
    Rad (.id (Multiplicative (ZMod 2))) {(inr (of ()) : GX (Multiplicative (ZMod 2)) Unit) ^ 2} ≠
      normalClosure {inr (of ())} := fun h =>
  absurd ((inr_of_mem_Rad_pow_iff ()).1 (h ▸ subset_normalClosure rfl) (.ofAdd 1) (by decide))
    (by decide)

end Torsion

/-! ### Centralizers as algebraic sets -/

section Centralizer

open FreeGroup
open scoped commutatorElement

/-- The centralizer of a set of coefficients is algebraic: the solutions of the commutator
equations `[x, m] = 1`, `m ∈ M`, in the variable `x` are the points whose `x`-coordinate
centralizes the image of `M` in `H`. -/
theorem V_image_commutatorElement (x : X) (M : Set G) :
    V ι ((fun m => ⁅(inr (of x) : GX G X), inl m⁆) '' M) = {v | v x ∈ centralizer (ι '' M)} := by
  ext v
  simp only [mem_V, Set.forall_mem_image, map_commutatorElement, eval_inr_of, eval_inl,
    commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_mul_comm, Set.mem_ofPred_eq, mem_centralizer_iff]
  exact forall₂_congr fun _ _ => eq_comm

/-- The solutions of the commutator equation `[x, g] = 1` in the variable `x` are the points whose
`x`-coordinate centralizes the image of the coefficient `g` in `H`. -/
theorem V_singleton_commutatorElement (x : X) (g : G) :
    V ι {⁅(inr (of x) : GX G X), inl g⁆} = {v | v x ∈ centralizer {ι g}} := by
  simpa using V_image_commutatorElement (ι := ι) x {g}

/-- Over a free group `F`, the solutions of the commutator equation `[x, a] = 1` for a free
generator `a` are the points whose `x`-coordinate is a power of `a`. -/
theorem V_singleton_commutatorElement_of {α : Type*} (x : X) (a : α) :
    V (.id (FreeGroup α)) {⁅(inr (of x) : GX (FreeGroup α) X), inl (of a)⁆} =
      {v | v x ∈ zpowers (of a)} := by
  rw [V_singleton_commutatorElement, MonoidHom.id_apply, centralizer_singleton_of]

namespace CoordGroup

variable (ι) in
/-- Coefficient-preserving homomorphisms to `H` from the coordinate group of the commutator
equation `[x, g] = 1` in one variable correspond to the elements of the centralizer of `ι g`, by
evaluation at `x`. -/
def gHomEquivCentralizer (g : G) :
    GHom (coeff ι {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX G Unit), (inl g : GX G Unit)⁆}) ι ≃ centralizer {ι g} :=
  (homEquiv ι _).trans <| (Equiv.funUnique Unit H).subtypeEquiv fun v => by
    simp [V_singleton_commutatorElement]

@[simp]
theorem gHomEquivCentralizer_apply_coe (g : G)
    (φ : GHom (coeff ι {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX G Unit), (inl g : GX G Unit)⁆}) ι) :
    (gHomEquivCentralizer ι g φ : H) = φ (inr (of ()) : GX G Unit) :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem gHomEquivCentralizer_symm_apply_mk (g : G) (h : centralizer {ι g}) (w : GX G Unit) :
    (gHomEquivCentralizer ι g).symm h
        (w : CoordGroup ι {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX G Unit), (inl g : GX G Unit)⁆}) =
      eval ι (fun _ => (h : H)) w :=
  (rfl)

/-- Over a free group `F`, coefficient-preserving homomorphisms to `F` from the coordinate group
of the commutator equation `[x, a] = 1`, for a free generator `a`, correspond to the powers of
`a`, by evaluation at `x`. -/
def gHomEquivZPowers {α : Type*} (a : α) : GHom (coeff (.id (FreeGroup α))
    {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit), (inl (of a) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit)⁆})
    (.id (FreeGroup α)) ≃ zpowers (of a) :=
  (gHomEquivCentralizer (.id _) (of a)).trans <| Equiv.subtypeEquivRight fun h => by
    rw [MonoidHom.id_apply, centralizer_singleton_of]

@[simp]
theorem gHomEquivZPowers_apply_coe {α : Type*} (a : α) (φ : GHom (coeff (.id (FreeGroup α))
      {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit), (inl (of a) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit)⁆})
      (.id (FreeGroup α))) :
    (gHomEquivZPowers a φ : FreeGroup α) = φ (inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit) :=
  (rfl)

@[simp]
theorem gHomEquivZPowers_symm_apply_mk {α : Type*} (a : α) (h : zpowers (of a))
    (w : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit) :
    (gHomEquivZPowers a).symm h (w : CoordGroup (.id (FreeGroup α))
        {⁅(inr (of ()) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit), (inl (of a) : GX (FreeGroup α) Unit)⁆}) =
      eval (.id (FreeGroup α)) (fun _ => (h : FreeGroup α)) w :=
  (rfl)

end CoordGroup

end Centralizer

/-! ### An inconsistent system

Over `F(a, b)` with one variable `x`, the system `{a, b, x}` has no solutions, and both its
radical and its normal closure are all of `F(a, b)[x]`. Its group is therefore trivial: the
coefficients do not embed in it and it has no coefficient-preserving homomorphism to `F(a, b)`. -/

section Inconsistent

open FreeGroup

/-- The system `{a, b, x}` has no solutions in `F(a, b)`. -/
theorem V_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_empty :
    V (.id (FreeGroup (Fin 2)))
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit)) = ∅ :=
  V_eq_empty_of_inl_mem (g := of 0) (by simp) (Set.mem_insert _ _)

/-- The radical over `F(a, b)` of the system `{a, b, x}` is all of `F(a, b)[x]`. -/
theorem Rad_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_top :
    Rad (.id (FreeGroup (Fin 2)))
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit)) = ⊤ :=
  Rad_eq_top_of_V_eq_empty V_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_empty

/-- The normal closure of the system `{a, b, x}` is all of `F(a, b)[x]`, which the system
generates. -/
theorem normalClosure_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_top :
    normalClosure
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit)) = ⊤ := by
  rw [eq_top_iff, ← GX.closure_image_inl_union_range_inr_of (closure_range_of (Fin 2))]
  exact (closure_mono (by simp [Set.range_subset_iff, Fin.forall_fin_two])).trans
    closure_le_normalClosure

/-- The group of the system `{a, b, x}` over `F(a, b)` is trivial. -/
theorem SystemGroup.subsingleton_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of :
    Subsingleton (SystemGroup (FreeGroup (Fin 2))
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit))) :=
  QuotientGroup.subsingleton_iff.2 normalClosure_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_top

/-- The coefficients `F(a, b)` do not embed in the group of the system `{a, b, x}`. -/
theorem SystemGroup.not_injective_coeff_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of :
    ¬Function.Injective (SystemGroup.coeff
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit))) :=
  SystemGroup.not_injective_coeff_of_inl_mem (of_ne_one 0) (Set.mem_insert _ _)

/-- The group of the system `{a, b, x}` has no coefficient-preserving homomorphism to
`F(a, b)`. -/
theorem SystemGroup.isEmpty_gHom_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of :
    IsEmpty (GHom (SystemGroup.coeff
      ({inl (of 0), inl (of 1), inr (of ())} : Set (GX (FreeGroup (Fin 2)) Unit)))
      (.id (FreeGroup (Fin 2)))) :=
  (SystemGroup.isEmpty_gHom_iff _ _).2 V_inl_of_inl_of_inr_of_eq_empty

end Inconsistent

end TauCeti.GroupAlgGeom
